<h2><b>What a Proper Base Actually Costs You in Time and Material</b></h2>
<p>Part of why sand-only feels appealing is that it looks like a meaningful shortcut, less digging, less material, less time. In practice, the difference is smaller than it appears. A standard gravel base runs 4 to 6 inches deep, which does mean additional excavation and material compared to a thin sand bed alone, but the compaction process itself, using a rented plate compactor, adds relatively little time to an overall project when weighed against redoing a failed sand-only installation a year or two later. Our<a href="/blog/how-thick-should-paver-base-be-for-a-walkway/"> guide to how thick paver base should be</a> breaks down exactly what that gravel layer involves and why the depth matters.</p>
<h2><b>What Happens If You Skip the Base and Regret It Later</b></h2>
<p>If you’ve already installed a walkway on sand alone and are seeing the predictable signs, dips, rocking pavers, edges spreading apart, the fix generally means lifting the affected pavers, excavating deeper to make room for a proper gravel base, compacting that base correctly, and resetting the pavers on top of it. This is essentially the same work as building it correctly from the start, just with the added step of removing and setting aside the pavers first. It’s a common repair scenario, and one that’s avoidable with a bit more effort at the outset.</p>
<h2><b>A Middle Ground: Can You Use Less Gravel Than Standard</b></h2>
<p>Some homeowners ask whether a thinner gravel base, say 2 inches instead of the standard 4 to 6, might be an acceptable middle ground between full base depth and sand alone. In practice, this doesn’t work as well as people hope. A base that’s too thin to properly distribute weight and resist compression behaves much more like sand alone than like a properly built base, since the structural benefit of a gravel base comes largely from having enough depth and compacted layers to actually do that job. If budget or time is the concern, it’s better to build a smaller walkway with proper base depth than a full-size one with an inadequate base.</p>
<h2><b>How to Tell If Your Specific Project Needs a Full Base</b></h2>
<p>If you’re unsure which category your project falls into, ask yourself a few honest questions. How often will this area actually be walked on, daily, occasionally, or almost never? Is it meant to last for years or just a season? Does it connect to other hardscape that people move between regularly? A yes to regular use, multi-year lifespan, or connection to other walked areas points strongly toward needing a proper gravel base rather than sand alone.</p>
<h2><b>Sand Type Still Matters, Even With a Proper Base</b></h2>
<p>It’s worth noting that even in a correctly built walkway with a full gravel base, the type of sand used for bedding and jointing matters. Coarse, angular bedding sand performs better than fine, rounded sand, since it compacts more predictably and resists shifting under the pavers. Polymeric sand for the joints, rather than plain sand, adds a binding quality once activated that plain sand simply doesn’t offer, helping resist weed growth and joint erosion over time. Neither of these sand choices substitutes for the gravel base underneath, but getting them right adds meaningfully to how well a properly based walkway performs.</p>
<h2><b>Why This Matters More in Colorado’s Climate</b></h2>
<p>Freeze and thaw cycles along the Front Range put extra stress on any structure that isn’t properly based, and this effect is even more pronounced on a sand-only installation. Water movement and expansion during freezing weather is one of the primary forces that shifts and unsettles ground-level structures, and a compacted gravel base is specifically designed to help manage that movement in a way sand simply isn’t built for. This is part of why we see sand-only walkways in this climate fail faster than similar installations might in a milder, more consistently dry region.</p>
<h2><b>When It’s Worth Talking to a Professional</b></h2>
<p>If you’re on the fence about whether your specific project needs a full base or can reasonably get by with sand alone, it’s worth a quick conversation with someone who can assess your actual site, soil type, and intended use rather than guessing based on general rules. What works fine for a stepping stone path through a garden bed is very different from what a daily-use entry walkway needs, and the line between those two isn’t always obvious without seeing the actual space.</p>
<p>With over 15 years of building and repairing walkways across a wide range of project sizes and soil conditions in the Denver area, our team at Ridgewell Landscape and Design can help you figure out what your specific project actually needs, whether that’s a full gravel base or a simpler sand-only approach for something smaller. Explore our<a href="/services/paver-patios/"> paver patio services</a> to see the kind of properly based, long-lasting work we build for full walkway projects.</p>

<h2><b>Frequently Asked Questions</b></h2>
<h3><b>Can I lay pavers directly on sand without a gravel base?</b></h3>
<p>Technically yes, but it’s only appropriate for small, light-use areas like garden stepping stones or temporary paths. Any regularly used walkway needs a compacted gravel base underneath for structural support.</p>
<h3><b>What happens if I use only sand for a main walkway?</b></h3>
<p>Expect uneven settling, rocking pavers, and edges spreading apart within a season or two, particularly with regular foot traffic and in a climate with freeze and thaw cycles.</p>
<h3><b>Is a thinner gravel base an acceptable compromise?</b></h3>
<p>Not really. A base that’s too thin to properly distribute weight behaves much more like sand alone than a properly built base, since the structural benefit comes from having sufficient depth and compaction.</p>
<h3><b>Can I fix a walkway I already built on sand alone?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, though the fix typically involves lifting the pavers, excavating for a proper gravel base, compacting it correctly, and resetting the pavers, which is essentially the same work as building it correctly from the start.</p>
<h3><b>Does the type of sand matter even with a proper gravel base?</b></h3>
<p>Yes. Coarse, angular bedding sand and polymeric jointing sand both perform significantly better than plain, fine sand, even when a proper gravel base is already in place underneath.</p>
<h3><b>Are there any situations where sand alone is actually the right choice?</b></h3>
<p>Yes, for small, low-traffic applications like garden stepping stones, potted plant areas, or temporary paths that aren’t expected to last multiple years or handle regular foot traffic.</p>

<h2><b>Ready to Build a Walkway That Actually Holds Up</b></h2>
<p>Sand alone has its place for small, low-stakes projects, but for anything that functions as a real walkway, proper base preparation is what determines whether it lasts for decades or needs redoing within a year or two.</p>
